language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ csplib_013_progressive_party_problem: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ A party is timetabled at a yacht club. Some boats are designated hosts. The crews of the other (guest) boats visit host boats for several successive half-hour periods: a host crew stays on board to act as hosts, while a guest crew visits several hosts in turn. Every boat can hold a limited number of people at once (its capacity), and crew sizes differ. The total number of people aboard a boat, its own host crew together with the guest crews visiting it, must not exceed its capacity. A guest boat cannot revisit a host, and two crews cannot meet more than once. Minimise the number of host boats.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `csplib_013_progressive_party_problem.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/csplib_013_progressive_party_problem.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `csplib_013_progressive_party_problem_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py csplib_013_progressive_party_problem.json
$   savilerow model.eprime csplib_013_progressive_party_problem_1.param -run-solver

$ n_boats: number of boats
given n_boats : int
$ n_periods: number of half-hour periods
given n_periods : int
$ capacity[b]: number of people boat b can hold at once, indices 1-based
given capacity : matrix indexed by [int(1..n_boats)] of int
$ crew_size[b]: number of crew members of boat b, indices 1-based
given crew_size : matrix indexed by [int(1..n_boats)] of int

$ is_host[b]: boat b is a host boat (boats are numbered from 1 here; the output numbers them from 0)
find is_host : matrix indexed by [int(1..n_boats)] of bool
$ visits[p,b]: the host boat (0-based index, as in the output) that boat b visits in period p; a host boat visits itself
find visits : matrix indexed by [int(1..n_periods), int(1..n_boats)] of int(0..n_boats-1)
$ num_hosts: the number of host boats (the quantity to minimise)
find num_hosts : int(0..n_boats)

$ search hints: decide which boats are hosts first, then the visits
heuristic sdf
branching on [is_host, num_hosts, visits]

minimising num_hosts

such that
$ the number of host boats is the number of boats designated as hosts
num_hosts = (sum b : int(1..n_boats) . toInt(is_host[b])),
$ a host boat's crew stays on board, so in every period a host boat is visited by itself (visits[p,b] = b - 1)
forAll p : int(1..n_periods) . forAll b : int(1..n_boats) . (is_host[b] -> visits[p,b] = b - 1),
$ a guest boat visits a host boat in every period
forAll p : int(1..n_periods) . forAll b : int(1..n_boats) . ((!is_host[b]) -> is_host[visits[p,b] + 1]),
$ a guest boat cannot revisit a host: the hosts it visits over the periods are all different
forAll b : int(1..n_boats) . ((!is_host[b]) -> allDiff(visits[..,b])),
$ the people aboard a host boat, its own crew and the guest crews visiting it, do not exceed its capacity
forAll p : int(1..n_periods) . forAll h : int(1..n_boats) . ((sum b : int(1..n_boats) . crew_size[b] * toInt(visits[p,b] = h - 1)) <= capacity[h]),
$ redundant: in each period the guest crews fit into the spare room of the host boats
forAll p : int(1..n_periods) . ((sum b : int(1..n_boats) . toInt(!is_host[b]) * crew_size[b]) <= (sum b : int(1..n_boats) . toInt(is_host[b]) * (capacity[b] - crew_size[b]))),
$ symmetry breaking: boats with the same capacity and crew size are interchangeable, so among them a host boat never comes after a guest boat
forAll i, j : int(1..n_boats) . ((i < j /\ capacity[i] = capacity[j] /\ crew_size[i] = crew_size[j]) -> (is_host[j] -> is_host[i])),
$ two crews meet at most once: for two boats, the number of periods in which they are at the same host is at most one
forAll i, j : int(1..n_boats) . ((i < j) -> ((sum p : int(1..n_periods) . toInt(visits[p,i] = visits[p,j])) <= 1))
